messages.reasoning_content:
`objectHashCode` is also a shared static with the same cross-class hazard: if the first hash call is on a class that overrides hashCode, then later calling hashCode on a different class would be invalid. Pre-existing.

Given all this, using `objectHashCode`/`hashCode()` on the class object for cache key is risky due to the shared static methodID issue. Using `System.identityHashCode` with its own cached static is equally subject to contacting the same static but System.identityHashCode methodID is on System class which doesn't override, so it's always valid and can be called with any object as argument since identityHashCode takes Object param. The methodID itself is from System class (constant, no override) so no cross-class hazard. The only hazard is if we cache the methodID once, it's always the same methodID on System.identityHashCode; valid forever. That's safe.

But the class object passed to identityHashCode doesn't need to be a specific type since identityHashCode takes java.lang.Object. So `CallStaticIntMethod(env, systemClass, systemIdentityHashCode, pyjob->clazz)` is always safe regardless of which class object.

But we still need `systemClass` jclass for CallStaticIntMethod. We can get it via FindClass each time (local ref) and not cache the jclass; only cache the methodID (which is valid because System class is never unloaded by bootstrap). Let's do:

```
static jmethodID systemIdentityHashCode = 0;

...

jclass systemClass = NULL;
jint classId;
if(systemIdentityHashCode == 0) {
    systemClass = (*env)->FindClass(env, "java/lang/System");
    if(process_java_exception(env) || !systemClass)
        goto EXIT_ERROR;
    systemIdentityHashCode = (*env)->GetStaticMethodID(env, systemClass, "identityHashCode", "(Ljava/lang/Object;)I");
    (*env)->DeleteLocalRef(env, systemClass);
    if(process_java_exception(env) || !systemIdentityHashCode)
        goto EXIT_ERROR;
}
systemClass = (*env)->FindClass(env, "java/lang/System");
if(process_java_exception(env) || !systemClass)
    goto EXIT_ERROR;
classId = (*env)->CallStaticIntMethod(env, systemClass, systemIdentityHashCode, pyjob->clazz);
(*env)->DeleteLocalRef(env, systemClass);
if(process_java_exception(env))
    goto EXIT_ERROR;

cacheKeyValue = PyInt_FromLong(classId);  // or PyLong_FromLong
cacheKey = PyTuple_Pack(2, pyClassName, cacheKeyValue);
Py_DECREF(cacheKeyValue);
```

But `PyInt_FromLong` is mapped to PyLong_FromLong in py3 via util.h macro. Great.

Actually we don't even need the class name in the key if we use identityHashCode? We should keep name too for readability / to avoid collision across different classes with same identity hash (extremely unlikely but keeps semantic clarity). Fine.

Let's also consider: Do we need to worry about `pyjob->clazz` being a jclass passed as jobject to CallStaticIntMethod? Yes, jclass is-a jobject. Good.

Now, using identityHashCode avoids the getClassLoader SecurityException concern and the shared objectToString hazard. This is a cleaner fix. Let's update the code.

